        "columns": [
          {
            "text": [
              {
                "type": "heading",
                "level": 3,
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "When we put life in the center of governance, what changes in our social practices?",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In a world marked by climate crisis and social inequalities, the concept of social biomimesis and bioregionalism emerges as a revolutionary proposal to rethink local governance. Oscar Gussinyer talks with Melina Ángel, an expert on biomimesis applied to social systems and a member of the Meraki organization in Colombia, together exploring how indigenous communities and ecosystems can inspire models of governance that put life in the center. ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "title": null,
            "titleHeading": null,
            "alias": "Text",
